# TestEchoServer drives the real runs. These cases need no client.
# With a count of 0 the server binds, prints the line and exits at once. The port is
# fixed, not 0, because the line names the bound port and stdout is compared
# byte for byte; both ports are below the Windows and Linux ephemeral ranges and the
# ports std's TestStdIo_EchoServer picks under WSL (20000 to 49999). The cases of one
# program run one after the other, so the dev and release runs of one target do not
# share the port at once.
#
# Each target has its own port, and no Linux run may ever bind the Windows port. With
# .wslconfig networkingMode=mirrored, a port a Linux process bound stays unbindable on
# the Windows host after the process exits cleanly, so one port for
# both targets fails the Windows runs with
# std.net.AddrInUse after any Linux run. The Windows case uses 18220, which no Linux
# case binds.
# The Linux case uses 18218: a port held on the host still binds inside WSL.
case no-connections-windows
target x86_64-windows
args 18220 0
stdout echo0-windows.out

case no-connections-linux
target x86_64-linux
args 18218 0
stdout echo0-linux.out

case bad-port
profile release
args x 1
stderr parse.err
exit 1
